AAPT (Android Asset Packaging Tool) is used to inspect and analyze Android APK resources, package information, manifests, and compiled resource tables.

When you run AAPT against an APK, it can inspect several types of files and information.

### 1\. Files inside an APK that AAPT can inspect

| APK file            | What AAPT can inspect                                                          |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| AndroidManifest.xml | Package, permissions, activities, services, receivers, providers, SDK versions |
| resources.arsc      | Compiled resource table, resource IDs, configurations and values               |
| res/                | Android application resources                                                  |
| res/layout/*.xml    | Layout resources and references                                                |
| res/values/*.xml    | Strings, colors, styles, dimensions and themes                                 |
| res/drawable/       | Drawable resources                                                             |
| res/mipmap/         | Application launcher icons                                                     |
| res/menu/           | Menu resources                                                                 |
| res/xml/            | XML resource files                                                             |
| res/raw/            | Raw resource entries                                                           |
| assets/             | Asset files (limited resource/package inspection)                              |
| META-INF/           | Not normally interpreted as Android resources                                  |
| classes.dex         | Not decompiled or analyzed as Java code                                        |
| lib/                | Native libraries are not analyzed internally                                   |
| kotlin/             | Not analyzed as Kotlin source code                                             |

Important: AAPT does not understand every file in an APK. It primarily understands Android's resource and packaging structures.

### 2\. AAPT commands to inspect an APK

Assume your APK is named `target.apk`.

1\. Display package information

```bash
aapt dump badging target.apk
```

2\. Display the manifest

```bash
aapt dump xmltree target.apk AndroidManifest.xml
```

3\. Display resource information

```bash
aapt dump resources target.apk
```

4\. Display resource configurations

```bash
aapt dump configurations target.apk
```

5\. Display APK strings

```bash
aapt dump strings target.apk
```

6\. Display XML file contents

```bash
aapt dump xmltree target.apk res/xml/example.xml
```

### 3\. What AAPT can help you discover during bug bounty

| Information                               | Security relevance                            |
| ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| Permissions                               | Identify sensitive permissions                |
| Exported activities                       | Potentially accessible application components |
| Exported services                         | Potential component exposure                  |
| Exported receivers                        | Potential broadcast attack surface            |
| Content providers                         | Potential data exposure                       |
| Deep links                                | Potential intent-handling weaknesses          |
| SDK versions                              | Identify outdated platform compatibility      |
| Debuggable flag                           | Identify debug configuration                  |
| Network security configuration references | Investigate network security settings         |
| Backup configuration                      | Investigate backup exposure                   |

Remember: discovering an exported component or permission does not automatically mean a vulnerability exists.

